From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 08:03:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: send iOS diagnostics to the log, never to the
 guest's standard error

The iOS port's start-up diagnostics were written with WriteFile to the
process parameters' hStdError: "[FEX-iOS] fast-write ENABLED" in
ProcessInit, and in every ThreadInit "ThreadInit() entered", the TI:
step markers, the DispatcherPatch lines and "ThreadInit() done", plus
the gs_cached and JIT pool FATAL lines. Logging.cpp fell back to the
same handle when __wine_dbg_output did not resolve. That handle is the
guest process's own: a pipe its parent reads, a file, a console. It
reached the app's log only when the launcher had handed the log file
over as the game's standard error.

PLA-403: a console probe ran "cmd.exe /c echo <marker>" with its
standard output and error in a pipe and read "[rpm-sink] alive", the
fast-write line and "ThreadInit() entered" there, and not the marker.
"ThreadInit() entered" was written as 32 bytes, its terminating NUL
included, so the C string the probe printed ended there; the TI:
markers, the DispatcherPatch lines, "ThreadInit() done" and cmd's
output followed in the pipe and were cut off by the NUL and the probe's
511-byte buffer.

Write them through Logging::IosDiagWrite: ntdll's __wine_dbg_output in
NUL-terminated pieces (it takes a C string; the old call passed the
buffer as is), the path LogMan's lines already take to the app's log.
Without __wine_dbg_output a line is dropped. Logging::Init also hands
rpmalloc its sink (rpmalloc patch of the same name), and ProcessInit's
flush of the deferred buffer now names it the deferred rpmalloc log,
since rpmalloc's early lines wait there too.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-19-child-stdout-pipe.md
Offered-upstream: no; the writes are the iOS port's (fex-port)
